"""Short, human-written chapter overviews shown at the top of each chapter MCQ page (static HTML)."""

OVERVIEWS = {
"ai-introduction-10": "Artificial Intelligence is the ability of machines to learn from data and make decisions that normally need human thinking. This chapter separates AI from ordinary automation, introduces the three domains of AI — Data Science, Computer Vision and Natural Language Processing — and looks at everyday uses such as voice assistants, recommendations and face unlock. Expect board questions that ask you to classify an application into its domain or to tell AI apart from simple programmed rules.",
"aldehydes-ketones-12": "Aldehydes and ketones both contain the carbonyl group (C=O), and its polarity explains almost everything in this chapter: nucleophilic addition, the reactivity order (aldehydes are more reactive than ketones), and the acidity of α-hydrogens. Board papers regularly test named reactions — aldol condensation, Cannizzaro, Clemmensen and Wolff–Kishner reductions — together with distinction tests such as Tollens', Fehling's and the iodoform test. Carboxylic acids and their acidity trends are part of the same unit.",
"application-derivatives-12": "This chapter turns differentiation into a problem-solving tool. You will use derivatives to find rates of change, decide where a function is increasing or decreasing, and locate local maxima and minima with the first and second derivative tests. Word problems on maximising area or volume and minimising cost carry high marks, so practise setting up the function carefully before differentiating. Tangents and normals are no longer in the rationalised syllabus.",
"arithmetic-progressions-10": "An arithmetic progression is a list of numbers with a constant difference between consecutive terms. The chapter rests on two formulas — the nth term aₙ = a + (n − 1)d and the sum Sₙ = n/2 [2a + (n − 1)d] — and on reading word problems about savings, instalments or rows of seats as an AP. Questions often ask for a missing term, the number of terms, or which term of the AP equals a given value.",
"biotechnology-12": "Biotechnology uses living organisms and their molecules to make useful products. The unit covers the tools of recombinant DNA technology — restriction enzymes, cloning vectors, PCR and gel electrophoresis — and their applications in medicine and agriculture, such as genetically engineered insulin, gene therapy and Bt crops. Board questions frequently ask you to explain a step in making recombinant DNA or to name the enzyme or vector used for a purpose.",
"chemical-kinetics-12": "Chemical kinetics studies how fast reactions go and why. The chapter defines rate and rate law, explains order and molecularity, and derives the integrated rate equation and half-life for first-order reactions. You will also use the Arrhenius equation to connect rate constant, temperature and activation energy, and collision theory to explain why heating speeds reactions up. Numerical questions on half-life and on calculating Ea from two temperatures are very common.",
"chemical-reactions-10": "This chapter teaches you to write and balance chemical equations and to recognise the main types of reactions: combination, decomposition, displacement, double displacement, and oxidation–reduction. Everyday effects such as corrosion of iron and rancidity of fats are explained using oxidation. Board questions often give an observation — a colour change, a gas evolved, a precipitate — and ask you to identify the reaction type and write the balanced equation.",
"coordination-12": "Coordination compounds contain a central metal atom or ion bonded to surrounding ligands. The chapter covers Werner's theory, IUPAC naming, coordination number, isomerism, and bonding through valence bond theory and crystal field theory, which explains colour and magnetic behaviour. Practise naming complexes, predicting the number of unpaired electrons, and deciding whether a complex is high-spin or low-spin — these appear in almost every board paper.",
"current-electricity-12": "Current electricity explains how charges flow through conductors. You will work with drift velocity, Ohm's law, resistivity and its temperature dependence, combinations of resistors and cells, Kirchhoff's laws and the Wheatstone bridge. Most board marks here come from circuit problems, so practise applying Kirchhoff's loop and junction rules systematically and finding equivalent resistance in series–parallel networks.",
"data-handling-10": "Data is the fuel of every AI system. This chapter explains how data is collected, cleaned and organised, and how charts such as bar graphs, line graphs, pie charts and histograms reveal patterns before a model is built. You will also meet simple statistical measures — mean, median and mode — and learn why missing or biased data leads to poor AI decisions.",
"data-structures-12": "A data structure organises data so that a program can use it efficiently. The CBSE syllabus focuses on the stack, a Last-In-First-Out structure, implemented in Python with a list: push using append() and pop using pop(). Board questions ask you to write push and pop functions, trace a sequence of operations, or apply a stack to reverse a string or check balanced brackets.",
"database-sql-12": "This chapter introduces relational databases — tables, records, attributes, primary and foreign keys — and SQL, the language used to manage them. You will write queries with SELECT, WHERE, ORDER BY, GROUP BY and HAVING, use aggregate functions, join two tables, and modify data with INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE. Output-prediction and query-writing questions on a given table carry significant marks.",
"differential-equations-12": "A differential equation relates a function to its derivatives. The chapter covers order and degree, forming the general and particular solutions, and three solution methods: variables separable, homogeneous equations (using y = vx) and linear first-order equations solved with an integrating factor. Most board questions ask you to identify the type of equation and solve it step by step.",
"dual-nature-12": "Light sometimes behaves as a wave and sometimes as a stream of particles. This unit covers the photoelectric effect and Einstein's equation, the de Broglie wavelength of matter, and the structure of atoms — Rutherford's model, Bohr's model and the hydrogen spectrum — along with nuclei, mass defect and binding energy. Numerical questions on work function, threshold frequency and de Broglie wavelength are frequent.",
"ecosystem-12": "An ecosystem is a functional unit of living organisms and their physical environment. This chapter covers productivity, decomposition, energy flow through food chains and food webs, ecological pyramids, and ecological succession. Expect questions on the 10% law of energy transfer, why the pyramid of energy is always upright, and the difference between gross and net primary productivity.",
"electrochemistry-12": "Electrochemistry links chemical reactions with electricity. The chapter covers galvanic cells, electrode potential and the electrochemical series, the Nernst equation, the relation between cell EMF and Gibbs energy, conductance and molar conductivity, Kohlrausch's law, electrolysis with Faraday's laws, batteries and fuel cells, and corrosion. Nernst equation numericals and questions on molar conductivity versus concentration are among the most frequently asked.",
"electricity-10": "Electricity in Class 10 covers electric current, potential difference, Ohm's law, the factors that affect resistance, resistors in series and parallel, the heating effect of current and electric power. Learn the formulas V = IR, R = ρl/A and P = VI = I²R = V²/R, and practise circuit problems — they form a large share of the Science paper.",
"electrostatics-12": "Electrostatics studies charges at rest. The unit covers Coulomb's law, electric field and field lines, electric dipoles, Gauss's law and its applications, electric potential and potential energy, and capacitors with and without dielectrics. Board papers often include a derivation using Gauss's law and numerical questions on capacitor combinations and stored energy.",
"em-induction-12": "Electromagnetic induction explains how a changing magnetic flux produces an EMF. The chapter covers Faraday's laws, Lenz's law, motional EMF, eddy currents, and self and mutual inductance, and leads into alternating current — RMS values, reactance, impedance, resonance in LCR circuits and the transformer. Lenz's law reasoning and LCR resonance numericals appear regularly.",
"first-flight-poems-10": "The poems in First Flight range from Robert Frost's short reflective pieces to humorous verse and poems about nature, freedom and childhood. Board questions test understanding of the central idea, poetic devices such as metaphor, personification and alliteration, and the poet's message. Reading each poem aloud and noting its tone and symbols is the most reliable way to prepare.",
"first-flight-prose-10": "The prose chapters of First Flight cover memorable stories and true accounts — from Nelson Mandela's long walk to freedom to light-hearted tales about everyday characters. Questions ask about themes, character traits, important events and the values each story conveys. Prepare short summaries of every chapter and practise answering extract-based and long-answer questions.",
"footprints-10": "Footprints Without Feet is the supplementary reader for Class 10 English. Its stories — science fiction, mystery and stories of human goodness — are tested through long-answer questions on plot, character and moral values. Keep a one-paragraph summary and two or three character sketches ready for each chapter.",
"genetics-evolution-12": "This unit covers Mendel's laws of inheritance, deviations from them such as incomplete dominance and co-dominance, sex determination, linkage, and genetic disorders, followed by the origin of life and the evidence and mechanisms of evolution. Expect cross-based questions (monohybrid and dihybrid ratios), pedigree analysis, and comparisons between Darwin's natural selection and other theories.",
"grammar-10": "Class 10 grammar focuses on tenses, modals, subject–verb agreement, determiners, reported speech, and active and passive voice, tested through gap-filling, editing and transformation exercises. The quickest gains come from learning the rules for reported speech and subject–verb agreement and practising a few editing passages under time pressure.",
"heredity-evolution-10": "Heredity explains how traits pass from parents to offspring. This chapter covers Mendel's experiments with pea plants, dominant and recessive traits, monohybrid and dihybrid crosses, and sex determination in humans. Board questions often ask you to predict the ratio of offspring from a cross or to explain why the father determines the sex of the child.",
"human-reproduction-12": "This chapter describes the male and female reproductive systems, gametogenesis (spermatogenesis and oogenesis), the menstrual cycle and its hormones, fertilisation, implantation, pregnancy and parturition. Diagram-based questions on the structure of the testis, ovary and sperm, and on the hormonal control of the menstrual cycle, are common in the board paper.",
"integrals-12": "Integration is the reverse of differentiation and also a tool for finding areas. The chapter covers standard integrals, integration by substitution, partial fractions and integration by parts, and definite integrals with their properties. Board papers include several integration questions, so learn the standard forms well and practise choosing the right method quickly.",
"life-processes-10": "Life processes are the basic functions that keep organisms alive: nutrition, respiration, transportation and excretion. The chapter explains photosynthesis, human digestion, aerobic and anaerobic respiration, the human heart and double circulation, transport in plants through xylem and phloem, and the structure of the nephron. Labelled diagrams of the digestive system, heart and nephron are frequently asked.",
"light-optics-10": "This chapter covers reflection by plane and spherical mirrors and refraction through glass slabs and lenses. You will use the mirror formula 1/v + 1/u = 1/f and the lens formula 1/v − 1/u = 1/f with the New Cartesian sign convention, calculate magnification and lens power, and draw ray diagrams. Ray diagrams and sign-convention numericals carry substantial marks.",
"matrices-12": "Matrices and determinants give a compact way to handle systems of linear equations. The unit covers types of matrices, matrix operations, transpose, symmetric and skew-symmetric matrices, determinants and their properties, adjoint and inverse, and solving equations using A⁻¹B. Questions on finding an inverse and solving a 3 × 3 system appear in almost every paper.",
"ml-concepts-10": "Machine learning is the part of AI in which computers learn patterns from data instead of following fixed rules. This chapter compares supervised, unsupervised and reinforcement learning, explains training and testing data, and introduces simple models used for classification and prediction. Expect questions that ask you to pick the right learning type for a real-life situation.",
"molecular-basis-12": "This chapter explains how DNA stores and expresses genetic information. Topics include DNA structure and packaging, the search for the genetic material, replication, transcription, the genetic code, translation, regulation of gene expression through the lac operon, the Human Genome Project and DNA fingerprinting. Diagrams of the replication fork and the lac operon are frequently asked.",
"networking-12": "This chapter covers how computers communicate: network types (PAN, LAN, MAN, WAN), topologies, transmission media, devices such as switches, routers and gateways, protocols like HTTP, FTP and TCP/IP, and web concepts including URLs and web servers. Case-study questions ask you to suggest the best layout, cable type and device for connecting the buildings of an organisation.",
"neural-networks-10": "Neural networks are AI models loosely inspired by the human brain, made of layers of connected nodes that learn by adjusting weights. The chapter introduces input, hidden and output layers, and then turns to AI ethics: bias in data, privacy, transparency and the responsible use of AI. Questions combine simple network concepts with real-world ethical dilemmas.",
"oop-python-12": "Object-oriented programming organises code around objects that combine data and behaviour. This chapter introduces classes and objects in Python, constructors (__init__), attributes and methods, and ideas such as encapsulation, inheritance and polymorphism. Please check your current syllabus: object-oriented programming is not part of the latest rationalised CBSE Computer Science syllabus, so treat this chapter as enrichment.",
"probability-12": "Class 12 probability builds on basic probability with conditional probability, the multiplication theorem, independent events, the theorem of total probability and Bayes' theorem, along with random variables and their probability distributions. Bayes' theorem questions — such as finding the chance that an item came from a particular machine — appear in almost every board paper.",
"python-basics-10": "Python is the most widely used language for AI. This chapter covers the basics needed in the AI course: variables, data types, input and output, operators, conditional statements, loops and lists. Board questions ask you to predict the output of short programs or to write a few lines of code for a simple task.",
"python-functions-12": "Functions let you write a block of code once and reuse it. This chapter covers defining functions, parameters and arguments (positional, default and keyword), return values, the scope of variables, and using built-in and user-defined modules such as math and random. Output-tracing questions and short function-writing tasks are common.",
"quadratic-equations-10": "A quadratic equation has the form ax² + bx + c = 0. The chapter teaches solving by factorisation and by the quadratic formula, and using the discriminant b² − 4ac to decide whether the roots are real and equal, real and distinct, or not real. Word problems on speed, area and ages that reduce to a quadratic are a regular feature of the board paper.",
"ray-optics-12": "Ray optics treats light as rays that travel in straight lines. The unit covers reflection by spherical mirrors, refraction, total internal reflection and its uses, refraction at spherical surfaces and lenses, the lens-maker's formula, combinations of lenses, prisms and dispersion, and optical instruments such as microscopes and telescopes. Derivations and numericals on lenses and prisms carry many marks.",
"real-numbers-10": "Real numbers include rational and irrational numbers. This chapter covers the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic, using prime factorisation to find the HCF and LCM, and proving that numbers such as √2 and √3 are irrational. The relationship HCF × LCM = product of two numbers is frequently tested in short-answer questions.",
"solutions-12": "A solution is a uniform mixture of two or more substances. This chapter covers ways of expressing concentration, Henry's law for gases, Raoult's law and ideal and non-ideal solutions, and colligative properties — relative lowering of vapour pressure, elevation of boiling point, depression of freezing point and osmotic pressure — together with the van't Hoff factor. Numerical problems on colligative properties are very common.",
"statistics-probability-10": "Statistics in Class 10 is about finding the mean, median and mode of grouped data using the direct, assumed-mean and step-deviation methods. The probability part covers classical probability for single events, with dice, coins and cards as typical examples. Practise the mode and median formulas for grouped data — they appear almost every year.",
"triangles-10": "This chapter is about similar triangles. You will learn the Basic Proportionality Theorem (Thales' theorem), the AAA, SSS and SAS criteria for similarity, and how to apply them to find unknown lengths. Proof of the Basic Proportionality Theorem and problems based on the similarity criteria are standard board questions.",
"writing-skills-10": "Writing skills in Class 10 English include formal letters (letters of complaint, enquiry and placing orders) and analytical paragraphs based on charts, graphs or cues. Marks are awarded for format, content, organisation and accuracy, so learn the correct letter format and practise turning data into a clear, well-structured paragraph.",
}
